625, 0.041534423828125, 0.0171356201171875, -0.0037364959716796875, 0.0149993896484375, 0.01503753662109375, 0.017669677734375, 0.0156097412109375, -0.0396728515625, -0.0269012451171875, -0.0029811859130859375, 0.002033233642578125, -0.0087738037109375, -0.0012493133544921875, 0.0255126953125, -0.0008630752563476562, -0.0011138916015625, -0.03814697265625, -0.007656097412109375, 0.036376953125, 0.037200927734375, 0.01271820068359375, -0.02520751953125, 0.024261474609375, -0.00928497314453125, 0.012725830078125, -0.01432037353515625, 0.00033473968505859375, -0.004611968994140625, -0.02630615234375, 0.01220703125, 0.0031185150146484375, 0.025634765625, -0.0025424957275390625, 0.043548583984375, 0.02362060546875, -0.0141143798828125, -0.0160980224609375, 0.004856109619140625, -0.006275177001953125, -0.006099700927734375, 0.0114593505859375, 0.003971099853515625, -0.0249786376953125, 0.00665283203125, -0.013580322265625, -0.013671875, 0.006683349609375, -0.0165863037109375, 0.0131072998046875, -0.0440673828125, -0.01219940185546875, -0.0071868896484375, -0.017913818359375, 0.00443267822265625, -0.04473876953125, -0.01070404052734375, 0.002002716064453125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. In practical deployments, such agents are expected to function reliably across a range of procedural variations, such as changes in task order or environment specification. However, their performance can be unexpectedly unstable under minor, non-adversarial changes in experimental conditions\u2014a phenomenon we term *fragility*.\n\n**Conceptual Distinctions**\n\nTo clarify terminology, Table 1 summarizes the key concepts used in this work:\n\n| Concept         | Definition                                                                 | Example Variation Tested | Operationalization in This Study                |\n|-----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Fragility       | Sensitivity of agent performance to procedural/contextual variations that do not alter the underlying task distribution | Task order, environment specification | Standard deviation of task completion rate >5% across 5 runs, or mean difference between best and worst orderings >10% |\n| Robustness      | Resistance to distributional shifts or adversarial perturbations            | Out-of-distribution tasks, adversarial noise | Not directly tested here                        |\n| Generalization  | Performance on unseen data drawn from the same or related distribution      | Held-out tasks          | Not directly tested here                        |\n| Instability     | Stochastic fluctuations or randomness in training                           | Random seeds, initialization | Not the focus; refers to unsystematic variance  |\n\nFragility, as defined above, is distinct from robustness and generalization, which concern distributional changes and unseen data, respectively. Instability refers to random fluctuations, whereas fragility is systematic sensitivity to procedural details.\n\n**Operational Thresholds:** An agent is considered \"fragile\" if the standard deviation of its task completion rate across five runs with different task orders exceeds 5% absolute, or if the mean difference between best and worst orderings exceeds 10% absolute. These thresholds reflect practical significance for reliability and reproducibility.\n\n**Motivation**\n\nStudying fragility is critical for two reasons: (1) In real-world applications, agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders or under slightly altered conditions, and high fragility undermines both user trust and reproducibility. (2) Prior work has focused on robustness and generalization, but the specific impact of procedural variations\u2014often overlooked\u2014can lead to misleading conclusions about agent reliability. Fragility thus represents a critical, underexplored failure mode that can persist even in otherwise robust or generalizable systems.\n\n**Contributions**\n\nThis work provides a quantitative assessment of fragility and its contributing factors in representative memory-augmented agents. The primary aims are: (1) to systematically quantify the fragility of memory-based self-improving agents under varying task orders and environments, and (2) to identify and evaluate factors contributing to this fragility, with a focus on task and environment specification. We hypothesize that (a) agent performance exhibits high variance and sensitivity to task order, and (b) underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to this fragility.\n\nMethods\n\n**Experimental Protocol Overview**\n\n1. **Agent Selection:** We evaluate two representative memory-based self-improving agents:\n   - Agent A: Retrieval-augmented language model (fine-tuned T5-Base) with a key-value memory bank storing (task description, solution, feedback) tuples.\n   - Agent B: Memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent (PPO backbone with LSTM-based memory module for textual feedback and solutions).\n   These architectures are widely used baselines in language modeling and reinforcement learning, respectively, and have been adopted in prior memory-augmented agent research [3,4]. While only two agents are studied, they represent distinct paradigms (supervised and RL) and memory mechanisms. We acknowledge this limits generalizability; future work should expand to additional architectures.\n\n2. **Environment Selection:** We use ALFWorld (household manipulation; 20 tasks) and BabyAI (navigation and instruction following; 15 tasks), chosen for their diversity in task structure, established use in benchmarking [5,6], and support for multi-step reasoning and language-based interaction. These environments cover distinct modalities but do not exhaust the space of possible tasks.\n\n3. **Baseline Agents:** For Agent A, the baseline is a T5-Base model without memory; for Agent B, a PPO agent with the memory module ablated. All agents are trained and evaluated under identical conditions.\n\n4. **Task Sampling:** Tasks are randomly sampled from the standard ALFWorld and BabyAI benchmarks, stratified by environment-provided difficulty ratings (easy, medium, hard) to ensure coverage (\u226530% per level).\n\n5. **Task Order Randomization:** For each run, the task sequence is shuffled using five fixed random seeds (42, 101, 202, 303, 404) to ensure reproducibility and sample plausible orderings.\n\n6. **Training and Evaluation:**\n   - Each agent is trained for 100,000 episodes per environment.\n   - Early stopping: Training halts if no improvement in average reward is observed on a held-out validation set (10% of tasks) for 10,000 episodes.\n   - Hyperparameter tuning: Grid search over learning rate [1e-5, 3e-5, 1e-4], batch size [8, 16, 32], memory size [50, 100, 200], and retrieval threshold [0.7, 0.75, 0.8]. Best configuration selected by average reward.\n   - For each task, agents are allowed up to 5 episodes to attempt completion.\n   - Performance metrics: Task completion rate (percentage of tasks solved) and average reward per episode.\n\n7. **Memory Analysis:** For each agent and environment, 60 memory entries (stratified by task type) are randomly sampled. Two independent, blinded raters annotate each entry using a rubric with three criteria:\n   - **Specificity:** How precisely the entry describes the task or solution (high: detailed and unambiguous; medium: partially detailed; low: vague).\n   - **Relevance:** How directly the entry applies to the current task (high: directly applicable; medium: partially applicable; low: unrelated).\n   - **Completeness:** Whether the entry contains all necessary information for successful execution (high: fully sufficient; medium: missing minor details; low: missing key information).\n   Inter-rater reliability (Cohen's kappa): specificity 0.89, relevance 0.85, completeness 0.88. Disagreements resolved via structured discussion.\n\n8. **Statistical Analysis Plan:** For each agent/environment, we report mean, standard deviation, and 95% confidence intervals for task completion rate and average reward across five runs. Differences across task orderings are assessed using one-way ANOVA (for >2 groups) or paired t-tests (for two groups), with Holm-Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons. Effect sizes (Cohen's d, \u03b7\u00b2) and sample sizes are reported for all comparisons.\n\n**Implementation Details:** Hyperparameters for all agents are detailed in Appendix Table 1. Experiments were conducted on NVIDIA V100 GPUs with PyTorch 1.10.\n\nResults\n\n**Fragility Quantification**\n\nTable 2 summarizes task completion rates and variance across five task orderings for each agent and environment.\n\n| Agent   | Environment | Mean Completion Rate (%) | Std Dev (%) | Best-Worst Diff (%) | Fragile? |\n|---------|-------------|-------------------------|-------------|---------------------|----------|\n| Agent A | ALFWorld    | 52.1                    | 7.8         | 19.0                | Yes      |\n| Agent A | BabyAI      | 61.4                    | 4.2         | 10.2                | Yes      |\n| Agent B | ALFWorld    | 48.7                    | 6.5         | 15.5                | Yes      |\n| Agent B | BabyAI      | 58.2                    | 3.9         | 8.7                 | Borderline |\n\nFor example, Agent A's completion rate on ALFWorld ranged from 42% to 61% across runs (n=5, F(4,95)=6.3, p=0.003, \u03b7\u00b2=0.21). Agent B showed similar variance. All agents except Agent B on BabyAI exceeded at least one fragility threshold.\n\n**Sensitivity to Task Order**\n\nRandomizing task sequences led to performance drops of up to 18% (mean difference: 13.2%, 95% CI: [8.1%, 18.3%], paired t-test, t(4)=4.1, p=0.014, Cohen's d=1.8, Holm-Bonferroni corrected). Variance increased further when the self-improving loop was enabled.\n\n**Memory Analysis**\n\nTable 3 presents summary statistics for memory entry quality (proportion of entries rated high/medium/low):\n\n| Agent   | Environment | Specificity (H/M/L) | Relevance (H/M/L) | Completeness (H/M/L) |\n|---------|-------------|---------------------|-------------------|----------------------|\n| Agent A | ALFWorld    | 0.55/0.32/0.13      | 0.62/0.28/0.10    | 0.48/0.37/0.15       |\n| Agent B | BabyAI      | 0.51/0.36/0.13      | 0.59/0.31/0.10    | 0.45/0.41/0.14       |\n\nEntries with low specificity or completeness were associated with lower task"}}